- SCOPE: Only request player_rolls when the player's action actually involves social interaction
  (talking, negotiating, gathering information, intimidating, bluffing). If the action is primarily
  combat or traversal (e.g. "run and attack", "move through the room"), return EMPTY player_rolls
  for the social domain.
- SOCIAL OWNERSHIP: This domain owns social skill checks and attitude consequences. It does NOT own
  weapon attacks, spell attacks, damage rolls, or combat attack prompts.
- CONSEQUENCES: When a witnessed action automatically changes NPC disposition, record that here as
  a social consequence (for example, an attitude shift from friendly to unfriendly).
- OPPOSED CHECKS: When the social mechanic is opposed (for example Bluff vs Sense Motive), use
  dc: 0 and mark the description as opposed because the target number is the other side's roll.
- DIPLOMACY: improving NPC attitude by one step requires a Diplomacy check. DC depends on current
  attitude: hostile DC 25+CHA mod, unfriendly DC 20+CHA mod, indifferent DC 15+CHA mod, friendly
  DC 10+CHA mod. Shifting two steps in one check increases DC by 10. Takes 1 minute of
  conversation (cannot rush in most cases).
- INTIMIDATE: demoralize in combat is a standard action vs DC 10+HD+WIS mod. Out of combat,
  forcing cooperation uses the same skill but the effect is temporary and the NPC becomes
  unfriendly afterward.
- BLUFF AND SENSE MOTIVE: opposed checks. Bluff DC modifiers: +0 believable, +5 unlikely,
  +10 far-fetched, +20 impossible. Sense Motive can also detect enchantment (DC 25).
- ATTITUDE TRACKING: note any attitude shifts in consequences so the context update step can
  track NPC dispositions accurately.
- GATHER INFORMATION: This is a use of the DIPLOMACY skill, NOT a standalone skill called
  "Gather Information". Always request a Diplomacy check (skill: "Diplomacy"). DC 10 for
  common knowledge, DC 20+ for obscure or protected information. Do NOT estimate how long
  gathering information takes — the TimeKeeper step handles time.
